\title{lexio: generalized input-output model with preemptive goal programming}
\description{
The model chooses the sectoral output \code{x} that best meets, in
lexicographic order, a set of goals: k satellite accounts declared by the
user (for instance, energy sources and labour) and the economic block ECO
(the final demand of every sector). The goals are written as
}
\details{
\deqn{G x + d^- - d^+ = g, \qquad G = \begin{bmatrix} D \ I - A \end{bmatrix},}

with deviations \verb{d^-, d^+ >= 0} and, optionally, a corridor on the
economic deviations, \verb{d_h^-, d_h^+ <= alpha_h |g_h|}. Each priority level
is an LP that minimizes the weighted sum of the penalized deviations of
its goals, with the earlier levels locked at their optima (\code{d_j == Z_j}).
By default the package runs the \verb{k!} orders of the satellite accounts,
with ECO fixed at the last level; the operator may list the orderings to
run (with several goals per level) and, only by an explicit declaration
(\code{economy = list(position = "free")}), move ECO to another level.

Typical workflow:
\enumerate{
\item declare the database you already have: technical structure (\code{Z}, \code{A}
